2026-05-19  Kamila Szewczyk  <k@iczelia.net>

	v1.3:  windowed Maurer universal statistical test (-eee,
	alongside LZ77F and the linear-complexity estimator).

	A count-only Maurer scorer (src/kernel/maurer.c) on the bit
	stream, MSB-first per byte.  L is fixed at 8 bits/block with
	Q = 10*2^L init blocks; the 2^L recency table is heap.  Each
	4 MiB absolute grid block runs a fresh-table Maurer producing
	an in-order Sum log2(distance) and a test-block count K; the
	per-block partials reduce in absolute block index order so the
	f64 statistic is bit-identical for any thread count, I/O mode
	and host.  L = 8 satisfies the NIST SP800-22 K >= 1000*2^L
	per-window validity with a 16x margin within a 4 MiB window
	(L = 16 would fall ~45x short); L/8 = 1 divides the grid.  The
	finalizer derives:
	  - maurer_fn: total Sum log2(distance) / total K.
	  - maurer_expected: the NIST SP800-22 expected value for
	    L = 8 (exact static constant; variance and the c
	    correction factor likewise, the latter via a libm-free
	    2^x so K^(-3/L) is bit-identical across hosts).
	  - maurer_deviation: headline z = |fn - expected| /
	    (c*sqrt(variance/K)), badged PASS / WEAK / FAIL.
	  - maurer_degenerate: highly repetitive stream (fn far below
	    expected), flagged like the linear-complexity note.
	No test block anywhere yields z = 0.0 (total function); NaN is
	the not-computed sentinel.  fastent_log2 only, the running sum
	fused through a volatile f64 barrier.  Drift versus a textbook
	whole-stream table-carrying Maurer is bounded and
	verdict-neutral (<= ~0.001%, measured 0.00073% on enwik8).

	Surfaced in the default, terse CSV, JSON, recursive and
	annotated output and a -H log2-distance histogram; sortable
	via --sort-by=maurer-deviation (which implies -eee).

	Scope: Maurer catches compressible / repetitive sources
	("how compressible under an idealised universal compressor")
	that survive order-0/-1, LZ77F and the linear-complexity
	screen; it is a global compressibility statistic, not a
	structural locator and not a substitute for the per-test
	advisories.

	v1.3:  windowed linear-complexity estimator (-eee, alongside
	LZ77F).

	A windowed GF(2) Berlekamp-Massey scorer (src/kernel/bm.c)
	reduces each whole 512-bit window to its linear complexity L;
	the finalizer derives:
	  - bm_mean_lc: mean per-window L; bm_mu: the NIST SP800-22
	    random-sequence expectation (libm-free).
	  - bm_deviation: headline z = |bm_mean_lc - bm_mu| /
	    sqrt(VarL/W), badged PASS / WEAK / FAIL.
	  - bm_chi / bm_chi_p: advisory NIST linear-complexity class
	    chi-square (6 bins, df 5), kept separate from the headline.
	  - bm_degenerate: near-constant stream (mean L below 2),
	    flagged like the LZ77F single-dominant-match note.
	Windows are 64 bytes and 64 divides the 4 MiB block grid, so a
	window never straddles a block: the integer sum-merge is
	bit-identical for any thread count, I/O mode and host with zero
	drift.  Pure integer GF(2) recurrence; FP only at finalize.

	Surfaced in the default, terse CSV, JSON, recursive and
	annotated output and the -H histogram; sortable via
	--sort-by=bm-deviation / bm-mean-lc (which imply -eee).

	Scope: GF(2) Berlekamp-Massey catches LFSR-class and low-bit
	linear recurrences (a maximal 16-bit LFSR scores L near 16) but
	not a truncated-high-byte LCG.

	A count-only LZ77F (acceleration 1, HLOG 13) match finder: it
	never emits a byte and keeps no output buffer.  It produces the
	exact count-only encoded size
	and three raw tables (per-offset match count, per-match-length
	count, LZ77-unmatched byte-value histogram), from which the
	finalizer derives:
	  - lz_cr_excess (S1): compressibility excess versus the
	    incompressible baseline outsz_rand(n).
	  - lz_lit_h / lz_lit_kl (S2): literal byte-value Shannon
	    entropy and its KL divergence to uniform (8 - H_lit).
	  - lz_match_cov (S3): fraction of bytes inside an LZ77 match.
	  - lz_off_conc: offset concentration 1 - H(off)/log2(65535);
	    lz_mlen_excess: mean match length minus MINMATCH.  Both
	    take their continuous 0 limit when fewer than two matches.
	  - lz_lit_chi: advisory literal chi-square versus uniform
	    (df 255) with its upper-tail p, kept separate from the
	    compressibility headline exactly as order-0 entropy and
	    its chi-square stay separate.
	  - lz_deviation: headline z from max(S1, S2/8, S3) over
	    sigma0 = max(2/n, 2^-7), badged PASS / WEAK / FAIL.
	  An exact-repeat / single dominant match (fewer than two
	  matches with high coverage) is flagged explicitly so a
	  zero offset/length concentration does not read as no
	  structure; the verdict still fails via S1/S3.

	The parse is keyed to a fixed 4 MiB block grid on the
	absolute file offset with a fresh hash table per block, so
	the count-only size and every table are exactly additive and
	the integer sum-merge is bit-identical for any thread count,
	I/O mode (mmap / stream / io_uring) and host.  Drift versus a
	whole-file table-carrying serial parse is at most about
	0.055 percent and verdict-neutral.

	Surfaced in the default, terse CSV, JSON, recursive and
	annotated output; new --sort-by columns lz-deviation, lz-cr
	and lz-match-cov (selecting one implies -eee).  With -eee -H,
	three extra plots follow the byte/bit plot: match offsets and
	match lengths in log2 buckets and the literal byte-value
	histogram (256 bins, title carries H_lit), using the same
	Unicode-glyph / --log / axis rendering.

2026-05-16  Kamila Szewczyk  <k@iczelia.net>

	v1.2:  extended statistics, FIPS 140-2 RNG self-tests,
	faithfully-rounded log2 family, yarg argument parser,
	histogram axis fixes, order-1 bigram.

	Extended statistics (-e / --extended), all derived at
	finalisation from the histogram and running sums at no
	per-byte cost, bit-identical across hosts and thread counts:
	  - min-entropy (H-infinity), collision entropy (H2) and
	    index of coincidence.
	  - poker test: 16-bin nibble chi-square, df = 15, generalised
	    from the byte histogram.
	  - variance / standard deviation, redundancy, distinct-symbol
	    count, most / least common symbol.
	  - per-bit-position bias P(bit k = 1): exposes structured
	    binary (dead high bits, ASCII bit 7) that order-0 entropy
	    and chi-square miss.
	  - surfaced in default / terse / JSON / recursive output;
	    new --sort-by columns; --annotate interpretive PASS / WEAK
	    / FAIL report with a headline verdict.

	Order-1 bigram (-ee, repeat -e):
	  - conditional entropy H(cur|prev) and adjacent mutual
	    information I(prev;cur); exposes order-1 structure the
	    order-0 measures and the linear SCC miss.
	  - runs (0/1 runs in bit mode, median-class runs in byte
	    mode), longest identical-symbol run, and the bit-mode
	    cusum max excursion of the +-1 walk.
	  - single scalar pass, deterministic, sum-mergeable; padded
	    256x256 u64 shadow planes per thread (byte mode, ~1 MiB),
	    2x2 in bit mode.  Split across the -j workers, the bigram
	    pair / runs / longest run / cusum stitched at the slice
	    boundary; stream / io_uring feed an SPMC pipeline.
	  - surfaced in default / terse / JSON / recursive output;
	    new --sort-by=cond-entropy / mutual-info columns.

	FIPS 140-2 RNG power-up self-tests (--fips-140-2):
	  - the 4.9.1 monobit, poker, runs and long-run tests over
	    each independent 20000-bit block, replacing the normal
	    analysis; pass/fail report, exit status 1 on any failed
	    block, 0 otherwise.
	  - blocks split across the -j workers; the verdict is
	    bit-identical regardless of thread count.  Not supported
	    with -r.

	Numerics:
	  - chi-square upper tail generalised to any odd df
	    (fastent_chisq_tail_df); df = 1 / 255 paths unchanged and
	    bit-identical.
	  - faithfully-rounded, libm-free fastent_log2 family
	    (fastent_log2, _ge1, _ratio, _fast) factored from the
	    entropy core; min / collision entropy use it directly
	    instead of the prior multiply-then-divide round trip.
	    Shannon entropy stays bit-identical.

	Command line:
	  - argument parsing moved to the vendored single-header yarg
	    (github.com/iczelia/yarg), fetched and SHA-256-pinned by
	    ./bootstrap so the zero-dependency / tarball build is
	    preserved.
	  - new short aliases -J/--json, -a/--annotate, -i/--io,
	    -l/--log, -C/--color.  -e/--extended is repeatable
	    (-ee = order-1 bigram).  --no-mmap removed (use
	    --io=stream); --color now requires an explicit mode.
	  - -j / --threads present only in builds with a thread
	    backend; rejected as unknown otherwise.
	  - all non --full-precision output kept within 80 columns;
	    histogram X-axis labels aligned under their ticks and a
	    Y-axis scale added.
	  - terse CSV gains a P-Exceed column (chi-square upper-tail
	    probability) after Chi-square, emitted with or without -e;
	    the default and JSON classic output stay byte-identical.

2026-05-15  Kamila Szewczyk  <k@iczelia.net>

	v1.1:  recursive mode, AArch64 NEON / SVE2, AVX-512 BITALG,
	io_uring (Linux) and IOCP (Windows), WebAssembly SIMD128,
	histogram visualisation, DOS / Win9x support, platform port
	abstraction.

	New analyse variants and runtime dispatch:
	  - AArch64 NEON (baseline) and ARMv7-A NEON (-mfpu=neon, with
	    softfp / hardfp ABI selection at configure time).
	  - AArch64 SVE2 with variable vector length, picked at runtime
	    via HWCAP2_SVE2 (AT_HWCAP2 on Linux, elf_aux_info on FreeBSD,
	    sysctlbyname("hw.optional.arm.FEAT_SVE2") on macOS).
	  - AVX-512 BITALG variant using VPOPCNTB for the bit-mode body;
	    F + BW remains the base tier for hosts without BITALG.
	  - WebAssembly SIMD128 body driven by wasm_i32x4_dot_i16x8 +
	    wasm_i8x16_popcnt; gated on -msimd128 at configure time.

	I/O:
	  - --io={auto,mmap,stream,uring} replaces the binary
	    --no-mmap toggle (which stays as an alias for --io=stream).
	  - io_uring backend on Linux 5.1+: four overlapping 2 MiB
	    reads via raw __NR_io_uring_setup / __NR_io_uring_enter,
	    no liburing dependency.  Falls back to stream on
	    pre-5.1 kernels (ENOSYS) unless the user forced --io=uring.
	  - IOCP backend on Windows Vista+: same four-slot pipeline
	    over overlapped ReadFile + GetQueuedCompletionStatus.
	    Selected by --io=uring on Windows for cross-platform
	    symmetry; ENOSYS under --with-windows-target=win95.

	Output:
	  - -H / --histogram: terminal block-plot of the byte (or bit)
	    distribution.  Glyphs selected per console: 8-level Unicode
	    blocks (U+2581..U+2588) by default, single-byte CP 437
	    (0xDC / 0xDB) on DOS / Win9x consoles, ASCII '#' / ' ' for
	    redirected output.  --color=auto|always|never picks dim /
	    default / accent foreground per byte class (control,
	    ASCII, high-bit).  --log toggles a logarithmic Y axis.
	  - -r / --recursive: walk a directory; emit one CSV row per
	    regular file (or a JSON array under --json).  Path column
	    plus the same six summary metrics as terse mode.
	  - --sort-by=COL[:asc|:desc] for recursive output where COL
	    is path, samples, entropy, chisq, mean, pi, scc.

	Platforms:
	  - WebAssembly via emscripten with -sSTANDALONE_WASM, for both
	    wasm32 and wasm64 (-sMEMORY64).  The wasm64 binary needs a
	    runtime with the memory64 proposal enabled (e.g. wasmtime
	    -W memory64).
	  - MS-DOS via DJGPP with CWSDPMI baked into the .exe.  Native
	    console output uses CP 437 block glyphs and DJGPP <conio.h>
	    textcolor / cputs for BIOS-attribute colour without ANSI.SYS.
	  - Windows 95 / 98 / ME via --with-windows-target=win95
	    (kernel32 + msvcrt only, PE subsystem 4.0, narrow path API).
	  - Linux on alpha, sparc64, sparc, m68k, hppa, arc, sh4 (glibc
	    static) added to the smoke-test matrix.
	  - OpenBSD pledge(2) support via AC_CHECK_FUNCS([pledge]):
	    "stdio rpath" before open, "stdio" after.

	Build / portability:
	  - Platform port layer (port-cpu, port-io, port-term, port-os,
	    port-thread, port-walk) replaces the previous tower of
	    #ifdef _WIN32 / __DJGPP__ inside fastent.c / analyze.c /
	    fastent-io.c.  Each port has a header plus per-platform
	    -posix.c / -win32.c (+ -djgpp.c for term, -arm.c / -x86.c /
	    -other.c for cpu).  Win32 implementations cross-compile under
	    mingw-w64; the Vista+ surface (SRWLOCK, CONDITION_VARIABLE,
	    CancelIoEx) is gated on !FASTENT_WIN_LEGACY.
	  - Thread pool migrated to a port (port-thread.h):  pthread on
	    POSIX, CreateThread + SRWLOCK + CONDITION_VARIABLE on
	    Win32 Vista+.  Win95 target keeps threads disabled.
	  - Windows native build picks up UTF-8 paths and a UTF-8
	    console: argv is reconstructed from GetCommandLineW with a
	    manual MSVCRT-compatible splitter, SetConsoleOutputCP /
	    SetConsoleCP set to CP_UTF8, file paths routed through
	    _wopen / CreateFileW.
	  - analyze-impl.h's V_* macro table broken out into
	    analyze-vec-{x86,neon,wasm}.h; the inner-loop template is
	    unchanged but the macro definitions for each ISA family now
	    live in their own header.
	  - Variant dispatcher is table-driven: one fastent_variant
	    table walked by a single pick function, replacing four
	    near-identical pick_*_variant helpers.

	Math:
	  - New fastent-math entropy-term function (p * log2(1/p))
	    based on a double-double Horner expansion + 128-entry
	    log-table, faithfully rounded, with no libm log dependency.
	    Replaces the previous libm log2 call so chi-square /
	    entropy output is bit-identical across libcs and FPUs.
	  - Split-domain rewrite (q = 1 - p exact via Sterbenz) avoids
	    cancellation when p is close to 1.

	Misc:
	  - TinyCC compatibility: no __atomic_* builtins, no
	    __builtin_fma along the hot path; io_uring backend uses
	    volatile-only ring pointers because io_uring_enter is
	    itself the kernel-side barrier.
	  - vzeroupper added at the AVX2 / AVX-512 tail to avoid SSE
	    state-machine penalties when the caller returns into
	    non-AVX code.
	  - Explicit POPCNT inclusion in the GCC builtin gate so the
	    bit-mode scalar walker doesn't fall through to libgcc's
	    __popcountdi2 out-of-line helper on -mssse3-only TUs.

2026-05-15  Kamila Szewczyk  <k@iczelia.net>

	v1.0:  Initial release of fastent.

	Tests: Shannon entropy, chi-square (with tail probability),
	arithmetic mean, Monte Carlo Pi, serial correlation.  Byte
	mode by default; bit mode under -b.  In-line case fold under
	-f, fused into the SIMD body.

	Outputs: human-readable text, CSV (-t), JSON (--json), with
	an optional per-value occurrences table (-c) and full
	precision rendering (-p).

	Throughput: 4-way banked SIMD histogram, sign-corrected
	PMADDUBSW serial-correlation cross-product, VPOPCNTB
	bit-mode popcount on AVX-512 BITALG hosts with a PSHUFB
	nibble lookup fallback, runtime CPUID dispatch across
	scalar / SSSE3 / SSE4.1 / AVX2 / AVX-512.  Memory-mapped
	input with MADV_SEQUENTIAL on regular files, 2 MiB read(2)
	loop otherwise.

	Threading: optional pthread worker pool (-j N / --threads),
	6-aligned slab partitioning, deterministic byte-identical
	merge.  -j auto resolves to one thread per online CPU.

	Platforms: Linux musl (x86_64, i386, aarch64, armhf,
	riscv64, ppc64le, ppc64, ppc, s390x, loongarch64, mips,
	mipsel, mips64, mips64el), Linux glibc (alpha, sparc64,
	sparc, m68k, hppa, arc, sh4), Windows (x86_64, i686,
	aarch64), Windows 95 (i686, kernel32+msvcrt only), macOS
	(x86_64, aarch64), MS-DOS via DJGPP with CWSDPMI bundled.

	Build: GNU autotools.  --enable-native, --enable-lto,
	--disable-threads, --with-windows-target=vista|win95.

	Testing: deterministic-fixture suite under make check,
	throughput benchmark sweep under make bench.
